    "title": "A Cure That Few Can Reach",
    "accessTier": "premium",
    "isFree": false,
    "xpReward": 50,
    "orderIndex": 30,
    "estimatedMinutes": 24,
    "description": "The arrival of gene-editing therapies for severe inherited blood disorders transforms what is medically possible while exposing the difficulty of delivering complex treatment equitably.",
    "intro": "The arrival of gene-editing therapies for severe inherited blood disorders transforms what is medically possible while exposing the difficulty of delivering complex treatment equitably.",
    "mission": "",
    "grammar": "",
    "phrases": [],
    "vocabulary": [],
    "dialogue": [],
    "reading": {
      "title": "A Cure That Few Can Reach",
      "text": "The arrival of gene-editing therapies for severe inherited blood disorders transforms what is medically possible while exposing the difficulty of delivering complex treatment equitably. The case belongs to a recognisably current debate, but the central task is not to repeat a headline. It is to determine which claim the available evidence supports, which causal mechanisms are plausible and whose interests or risks may disappear inside an average.\n\nThe strongest finding is that A therapy can be scientifically transformative and still have limited population impact if diagnosis, specialist centres, financing and long-term follow-up remain scarce. This matters because public discussion often treats a visible outcome as if it had a single cause. Scientific and social analysis instead asks how systems interact, what is being compared and whether the measurement captures the concept that policy is supposed to address.\n\nA genuine tension complicates the response: High initial prices may reflect research and manufacturing complexity, but they can also shift risk onto health systems and exclude countries carrying a large disease burden. The second position does not erase the first. It identifies a cost, trade-off or distributional effect that a technically successful intervention may create. Acknowledging that objection should refine the argument rather than reduce it to “both sides” without judgement.\n\nThe most defensible response is therefore institutional as well as individual: Outcome-based financing, technology transfer, regional treatment centres and continued investment in simpler therapies should be considered together. Such a programme requires measurable objectives, responsible organisations and a way to revise decisions when outcomes differ from expectations. It also requires language precise enough to separate observed results from forecasts and ethical preferences.\n\nOne limitation remains: Early clinical success does not remove uncertainty about durability, late effects or access beyond highly selected patients. The conclusion is consequently conditional rather than weak. A responsible reader can support action while specifying uncertainty, monitoring unequal effects and stating what future evidence would justify a different decision. That combination of judgement and revisability defines advanced scientific-social literacy.",
      "questions": [
        "What analytical problem does “A Cure That Few Can Reach” establish?",
        "Which finding anchors the argument in “A Cure That Few Can Reach”?",
        "Which trade-off qualifies the main argument about gene editing, treatment and access?",
        "Which institutional response does the text defend, and why?",
        "How does the stated limitation affect the conclusion of “A Cure That Few Can Reach”?"
      ],
      "references": [
        {
          "author": "World Health Organization",
          "title": "Human genome editing: ensuring responsible use of life sciences research",
          "year": 2025,
          "url": "https://www.who.int/health-topics/human-genome-editing/ensuring-responsible-use-of-life-sciences-research"
        }
      ]
    },
