    "dialogue": [],
    "reading": {
      "title": "The Price Behind Cheap Fashion",
      "text": "Fast Fashion and Hidden Costs is often discussed through striking examples, but a responsible account begins by separating an individual case from the wider system around it. The available evidence points to a concrete public question: how are decisions made, who experiences their consequences, and which assumptions shape the first explanation people hear?\n\nOne position argues that low prices make fashion accessible. Supporters can point to practical evidence, yet the strength of that evidence depends on what has been measured, whose experience has been included and whether a short-term result can be treated as a long-term pattern. A persuasive example is not automatically a complete explanation.\n\nA competing position holds that hidden labour and environmental costs are shifted elsewhere. This objection does not cancel the first claim; it tests its limits. It asks whether costs have been shifted elsewhere, whether some groups have less choice than others, and whether a solution that appears efficient in one context produces a different result in another.\n\nThe most useful reporting therefore compares sources, distinguishes correlation from cause and makes uncertainty visible. It also avoids turning a social issue into a simple moral story about one consumer, worker, institution or community. Evidence has to be interpreted alongside rules, incentives and unequal access to information or resources.\n\nA defensible direction is to buy less, demand traceability and improve regulation. This is not a final answer that removes disagreement. It is a proposal that can be assessed against clear criteria: transparency, fairness, practical effect and the quality of the evidence available. If stronger evidence changes the balance of reasons, the conclusion should change with it.",
      "questions": [
        "What specific event introduces the debate in “The Price Behind Cheap Fashion”?",
        "What evidence supports the claim that low prices make fashion accessible?",
        "How does the text respond to the concern that hidden labour and environmental costs are shifted elsewhere?",
        "Why does the article support the following direction: buy less, demand traceability and improve regulation?",
        "What new evidence could reasonably change the conclusion in “The Price Behind Cheap Fashion”?"
      ]
    },
